5. GOVERNANCE MODEL

Implement federated governance.

The central association establishes common standards, while authorised class set executives manage their own activities within those standards.

The central association must not need to perform routine class set operations on behalf of every class set.

At the same time, class set executives must not be able to bypass central policies, tenant security, financial controls, or mandatory reporting requirements.

Implement three principal governance scopes:

Platform scope

Controls platform-level configuration, tenant provisioning, technical administration, and platform security.

Platform technical administrators must not automatically have unrestricted access to private welfare records or confidential communications.

Association scope

Controls association-wide policies, class set recognition, governance standards, authorised oversight, association-wide reporting, and escalation procedures.

Class set scope

Controls the class set's authorised membership workflows, leadership, communications, meetings, events, projects, and financial activities within delegated authority.

Every privileged operation must verify the user's current role, scope, and permissions on the server.

Executive permissions must support term dates, suspension, revocation, and orderly succession.

A person may hold multiple offices, but permissions must be explicitly granted and auditable.

